From 631c6fbe56a3b740be290a49709d0e90128c6d38 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 05:59:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][PlayerInfo] The plan of the speakers says what gets
 sound, what the receiver declares and renders, and the downmix

The plan of the speakers on the Audio page lit what the stream's layout
or Atmos bed names, and drew a faint ring for every other place: it
said nothing of the TV or receiver, of the objects that a receiver
renders, of the channels that Hades sends when it decodes, or of a
downmix. A 5.1 of Kodi's channels (BL/BR) was drawn at the back, an
Atmos bed of 5.1 at the sides.

Each place now shows one of four things (SpeakerPlan.h), with a legend
under the plan:

- a disc: it gets sound. Passed through, the stream's bed or channels;
  decoded, the channels that the sink was opened with, which Hades
  sends.
- a ring of the same light: Dolby Atmos passed through to a receiver
  that declares it (E-AC-3 with Joint Object Coding, or MAT with object
  audio, in its short audio descriptors), which renders the objects to
  every speaker it has: a speaker that it declares beyond the bed.
- a grey ring: the TV or receiver declares it, and nothing goes to it.
- the faint ring of the plan: nothing is known of it.

What a TV or receiver declares is the Speaker Allocation Data Block of
the EDID of the display that Kodi shows on, read only where the sink
opened the output of that display (its ELD names the same display). The
ELD carries the first byte of that block alone (drm_edid.c: eld[7] =
data[0]), no height, and the HDA HDMI codec shows a display that
declares none as having every speaker. The bits are read as edid-decode
and libdisplay-info read them; a receiver of CTA-861-D declares 5.1
with RL/RR (its surrounds) and 7.1 with RLC/RRC as well (its backs). Of
DTS:X nothing is taken: the bit that edid-decode reads for it comes
from reverse engineering. Kodi's ELD parser reads that first byte by
the bits of drm_eld.h, as it should.

New rows of the page say it in words: the layout of the channels sent
and what a downmix folds into what (by the matrix that libswresample
builds for the decoder's layout and the sink's, with Kodi's levels:
"FC > FL+FR; BL+BR > FL+FR", "LFE left out" where
audiooutput.mixsublevel is 0), and the speakers declared, the display
that declares them ("5.1.2" and its name, or why none are known) and
what becomes of the objects. The plan has a
place for the top middle pair (TpSiL/TpSiR). The line of kodi.log of a
stream that names no speaker stays.

Strings: Estuary #54935, #54937 to #54940, #54942 to #54946 and #54948 to #54951, in English and in the three
Spanish translations of Estuary. The pages for other skins
(system/hades) are made again from Estuary's files.
